Thanks for the YANI, it's one of my favorite parts of rgrn.

> Rage would be an involuntary reaction. Some characters would be more
> prone to it than others, and there would probably be items that bring
> it on. Some races might have strong racial animosites that can induce
> rage (Dwarf/ goblin, elf/orc) and getting hurt in battle can bring it
> on as well. Maybe the loss of a well-loved pet might be enough in some
> cases. The kind of rage I have in mind for this YANI would be
> well-described by the beserker frenzies shown in "Slaine the Barbarian"
> or TGTNHIN. 
It should be voluntary as well as involuntary (for the barbarian at 
least), perhaps trained as a skill (each time you enter a rage is 
equivalent to x hits or something).  It should also be available as a 
spell (for both players and mosters) so that a wizard can turn a throne 
room or a barracks into utter chaos.

> Anyway, when in a rage, the character gains unnatural strength and
> extra attacks (Bite, kick, headbutt).
That's good.

> - Nutrition use is increased.
Makes sense.

> - In extreme beserker rages, the character's body temperature can
> increase to such an extent that it does fire damage to inventory.
That's harsh, perhaps fire resistance negates this effect?  At any rate 
if you are hot enough to ignite your inventory you should be doing fire 
damage to your enemies.

> - Body armour may be ripped open in some extreme frenzies (Hulk smash!)
This is really nasty too, perhaps just tearing off and dropping the 
armor instead.

> - The character may drop any wielded weapon (including cursed items!)
> and attack bare handed.
> - The character may throw random inventory items or items on the floor
> at monsters (even going so far as to strip armour off and throw that,
> in some rare cases)
Dropping the weapon doesn't seem as plausible.  Hurling it at an enemy 
is much more likely, but that should probably only happen if they're not 
adjacent to you.  At any rate when chucking random items the weapons 
should go first.

> - If there is a target adjacent suitable for attack, the game will pick
> one of them and attack it no matter what input you try to enter into
> the keyboard.
> - The player will not be in full control of the character's movement:
> If you attempt make a move away targets, or perform any other action,
> there is a strong chance the character will move towards a target
> instead.
> - Targets for attack include, in order of priority, hostile monsters,
> peaceful and tame monsters, altars, sinks and walls. Biting, kicking
